  <!-- Main content -->
  <main class="min-h-screen pt-32 px-6 sm:px-16 md:px-24 lg:px-32 pb-24">

    <!-- Back link -->
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto mb-10">
      <a
        href="projects.html"
        class="text-sm tracking-widest uppercase hover:underline"
      >
        ← Back to Projects
      </a>
    </div>

    <!-- Page heading -->
    <header class="max-w-5xl mx-auto text-center mb-20">

      <h1 class="text-4xl font-light tracking-wide uppercase">
        Project Consultant
      </h1>

      <p class="mt-4 text-sm tracking-widest uppercase opacity-70">
        IVS Advisors
      </p>

    </header>

    <!-- Projects -->
    <div class="max-w-5xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-24">

      <!-- Project 1 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">
          Boys &amp; Girls Clubs of Lanier
        </h2>

        <p class="text-sm tracking-widest uppercase opacity-60 mb-8">
          Gainesville, Georgia · Closed April 9, 2025
        </p>

        <div class="text-base leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-5">
          <p>
            From a humble start in 1954, Boys &amp; Girls Clubs of Lanier has
            grown into an important resource for youth across northeast Georgia.
            The organization now supports twenty-five clubs across two counties,
            helping underserved young people access a supportive after-school
            learning environment.
          </p>

          <p>
            New Markets Tax Credit proceeds helped finance the rehabilitation
            and overhaul of One Positive Place, a 25,000-square-foot club.
            Carver State Bank and Truist Community Capital provided allocation,
            with Truist also serving as the tax credit investor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <img
          src="static/cprojone.png"
          alt="Boys and Girls Clubs of Lanier project"
          class="w-full h-auto mt-10 rounded-md shadow-md object-cover"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Project 2 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">
          $15 Million FPL Food Expansion
        </h2>

        <p class="text-sm tracking-widest uppercase opacity-60 mb-8">
          Thomasville, Georgia · Closed December 27, 2024
        </p>

        <div class="text-base leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-5">
          <p>
            FPL Food began in 2004 with the purchase of a harvesting and
            grinding facility. Over the following two decades, the company
            expanded its operations while pursuing its goal of producing
            sustainable, high-quality beef.
          </p>

          <p>
            New Markets Tax Credit proceeds supported an expansion of the
            company’s rural facility, including a new dock and cooling
            facilities. The improvements increased freight capacity, storage,
            operational efficiency, inventory control, and processing
            capabilities.
          </p>

          <p>
            Georgia Community Reinvestment Fund provided the NMTC allocation,
            while a consortium of local Georgia banks served as the tax credit
            investor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <img
          src="static/cprojtwo.png"
          alt="FPL Food expansion project"
          class="w-full h-auto mt-10 rounded-md shadow-md object-cover"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Project 3 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">
          $11 Million Newberry College Project
        </h2>

        <p class="text-sm tracking-widest uppercase opacity-60 mb-8">
          Newberry, South Carolina · Closed August 30, 2024
        </p>

        <div class="text-base leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-5">
          <p>
            Newberry College is an accessible college located in a rural South
            Carolina community. The school combines a liberal arts foundation
            with professional degree programs and emphasizes personal attention
            and a close-knit academic environment.
          </p>

          <p>
            NMTC proceeds funded upgrades to the Nursing and Health Sciences
            Center, including classrooms and simulation laboratories. The
            financing also supported a new healthcare clinic and renovations to
            the athletic stadium and its field house.
          </p>

          <p>
            South Carolina Community Loan Fund provided allocation, and U.S.
            Bank served as the tax credit investor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <img
          src="static/cprojthree.png"
          alt="Newberry College project"
          class="w-full h-auto mt-10 rounded-md shadow-md object-cover"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Project 4 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">
          $19.5 Million Neighborhood Health Project
        </h2>

        <p class="text-sm tracking-widest uppercase opacity-60 mb-8">
          Fort Wayne, Indiana · Closed December 12, 2023
        </p>

        <div class="text-base leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-5">
          <p>
            Neighborhood Health was developed to address serious healthcare
            barriers affecting southeast Fort Wayne, including limited access
            to quality healthcare, behavioral health services, oral care, and
            multilingual services.
          </p>

          <p>
            The organization acquired the former L.C. Ward building and
            transformed the 40,000-square-foot property into a state-of-the-art,
            full-service community health center. New Markets Tax Credits helped
            make the redevelopment possible, with four CDEs providing
            allocation.
          </p>

          <p class="font-medium">
            The project received the 2024 Operating Business QLICI of the Year
            national award.
          </p>
        </div>

        <img
          src="static/cprojfour.png"
          alt="Neighborhood Health project"
          class="w-full h-auto mt-10 rounded-md shadow-md object-cover"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Project 5 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">
          $4 Million GrowFood Carolina Project
        </h2>

        <p class="text-sm tracking-widest uppercase opacity-60 mb-8">
          Charleston, South Carolina · Closed May 25, 2023
        </p>

        <div class="text-base leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-5">
          <p>
            Sponsored by the South Carolina Coastal Conservation League,
            GrowFood Carolina developed a 12,000-square-foot storage and
            distribution center designed to strengthen the regional rural
            economy.
          </p>

          <p>
            The organization connects small farms with the growing local food
            movement by providing sales, marketing, logistics, warehousing, and
            distribution services that are often only available to large-scale
            agricultural operations.
          </p>

          <p>
            This small-dollar NMTC project produced a $993,000 subsidy to
            support local farmers, community outreach, and educational programs.
            Truist Community Capital served as both investor and CDE.
          </p>
        </div>

        <img
          src="static/cprojfive.png"
          alt="GrowFood Carolina project"
          class="w-full h-auto mt-10 rounded-md shadow-md object-cover"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Project 6 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">
          $20 Million HUB for Community Innovation
        </h2>

        <p class="text-sm tracking-widest uppercase opacity-60 mb-8">
          Augusta, Georgia · Closed October 14, 2021
        </p>

        <div class="text-base leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-5">
          <p>
            The HUB for Community Innovation was sponsored by the Medical
            College of Georgia Foundation, the Community Foundation for the
            CSRA, and the Boys &amp; Girls Clubs of Greater Augusta.
          </p>

          <p>
            The project brought together several nonprofit organizations serving
            Augusta’s Laney Walker neighborhood. Two new facilities created more
            than 49,000 square feet of space for free healthcare, adult literacy
            services, a farmers’ market, child watch services, and after-school
            support for children and teenagers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carver State Bank, Truist Community Capital, and Community
            Reinvestment Fund provided allocation, with Truist serving as the
            tax credit investor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <img
          src="static/cprojsix.png"
          alt="HUB for Community Innovation project"
          class="w-full h-auto mt-10 rounded-md shadow-md object-cover"
        />
      </section>

      <!-- Project 7 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-light tracking-wide uppercase mb-3">
          $10 Million Boys &amp; Girls Clubs of Greater Augusta
        </h2>

        <p class="text-sm tracking-widest uppercase opacity-60 mb-8">
          Augusta, Georgia · Closed January 30, 2019
        </p>

        <div class="text-base leading-relaxed flex flex-col gap-5">
          <p>
            Boys &amp; Girls Clubs of Greater Augusta provides after-school and
            summer programming for approximately 3,900 young people between the
            ages of six and eighteen in the Augusta-Richmond County area.
          </p>

          <p>
            The NMTC transaction supported the ground-up construction of two
            facilities serving low-income children and families in a severely
            distressed census tract. The expansion increased capacity across the
            two locations by more than 300 students per day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Carver State Bank and Dudley Ventures provided financing, while
            Valley National Bank served as the tax credit investor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <img
          src="static/cprojseven.png"
          alt="Boys and Girls Clubs of Greater Augusta project"
          class="w-full h-auto mt-10 rounded-md shadow-md object-cover"
        />
      </section>

    </div>
